The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!RB_EMPTY_ROOT(&msk->out_of_order_queue))
    rcvwin += MPTCP_SKB_CB(msk->ooo_last_skb)->end_seq - msk->ack_seq;
```
```
if (sk->sk_socket)
    mptcp_rcvbuf_grow(sk, msk->rcvq_space.space);
```
Reason:
The first statement incorrectly adds the MPTCP-level out-of-order queue length to the receive window estimate in `mptcp_rcvbuf_grow()`. Since OoO data is physiological when multiple subflows are active and does not indicate drops or retransmissions, this leads to the receive buffer drifting toward `tcp_rmem[2]`, wasting memory. The second statement calls `mptcp_rcvbuf_grow()` whenever OoO data is queued (in `mptcp_data_queue_ofo()`), which can occur before receive space initialization is complete (e.g., `msk->ack_seq` may be zero). This triggers a divide-by-zero Oops in the earlier `do_div(grow, oldval)` when `oldval` (copied from `msk->rcvq_space.space`) is zero, due to a subtle race condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
Remove the line:
```
if (!RB_EMPTY_ROOT(&msk->out_of_order_queue))
    rcvwin += MPTCP_SKB_CB(msk->ooo_last_skb)->end_seq - msk->ack_seq;
```
Remove the line:
```
if (sk->sk_socket)
    mptcp_rcvbuf_grow(sk, msk->rcvq_space.space);
```
Reason:
Removing these lines eliminates the incorrect accounting of MPTCP-level OoO data in receive buffer auto-tuning, preventing the receive buffer from unnecessarily growing toward `tcp_rmem[2]`. It also removes the call to `mptcp_rcvbuf_grow()` from the OoO datapath, thus closing the race condition where a divide-by-zero could occur if receive space initialization was not yet complete when the first OoO data arrived.

